<?php

return [
    ['A morning urine specimen is delivered to the laboratory in a container that has been kept at room temperature for four hours.', 'The first-instance result most likely to be spuriously affected by the delay and the room-temperature storage is which measure?', 'easy', 'C', 'Color', 'Clarity', 'Nitrite and urobilinogen (and the leukocyte esterase and pH by bacterial multiplication)', 'Protein', 'Keeping urine at room temperature allows bacterial overgrowth that can raise the nitrite, lower the pH, and consume glucose and ketones, while the cells and casts lyse; the refrigerated sample, tested within two hours, preserves the constituents. Refrigeration before analysis is the standard handling.'],
    ['A urine sample is received that is markedly cloudy; the technologist performs the examination and then the microscopic.', 'A cloudy urine specimen that clears when warmed but becomes cloudy again on cooling contains which material?', 'advanced', 'A', 'Amorphous urates (and phosphates may persist as white sediment cold or warm)', 'Many red cells', 'Bacteria only', 'Fat globules', 'Amorphous urates and phosphates produce a sediment that dissolves when the specimen is warmed and reprecipitates on cooling; the distinction from pathologic sediment comes from the microscopic examination, which shows the amorphous material rather than cells or casts. Red cells, bacteria, and fat appear differently in the microscopy.'],
    ['A dipstick is dipped into a fresh urine sample, and the chemical pads are read at the correct timing.', 'The chemical reagent strip changes the color for a positive test through which principle, and which pad is most affected by the reading time?', 'intermediate', 'C', 'The radioactivity signal', 'The density change', 'A dye-coupled oxidation or pH-dependent reaction, and the readings are taken at the strip-specified interval because the pads change color over time', 'The viscosity change', 'The urine reagent pads use enzyme and chemical dye reactions (peroxidase with glucose, diazo with bilirubin) that develop color over a defined interval; reading early or late falsifies the low-positives, and the bilirubin and urobilinogen pads are especially time-sensitive. The pad timing is specified per product.'],
    ['A patient has a strongly positive glucose on the dipstick with a high specific gravity; the physician asks whether the measurement is reliable.', 'The glucose oxidase pad on the dipstick is based on which reaction, and the ascorbic acid interference risk appears in which setting?', 'intermediate', 'B', 'A reducing reaction', 'Glucose oxidase producing hydrogen peroxide that reacts with the chromogen, with false negatives at high ascorbic acid levels', 'An acid dye', 'A pH change', 'The glucose pad depends on glucose oxidase generating peroxide, which oxidizes the chromogen in a peroxidase step; vitamin C (ascorbic acid) and ketones compete for the peroxide and can produce a false negative, while the pad also misses the non-glucose sugars detected by the reducing (Benedict) methods. The reaction chemistry explains both the specificity and the interference.'],
    ['A newborn has a urine that reduces Benedict reagent strongly but has a negative glucose oxidase pad.', 'A urine with reducing power that is negative for glucose by the enzyme pad, in a newborn with galactosemia, is explained by which mechanism?', 'advanced', 'D', 'Contamination of the specimen', 'An antioxidant', 'Bacterial oxidase', 'The reducing (copper reduction) method detects galactose and other reducing sugars, while the glucose oxidase pad detects only glucose', 'The Benedict-type reducing test detects any reducing sugar, and in a galactose-fed newborn the galactose yields the reduction while the glucose-specific pad stays negative; the finding is classic for galactosemia. The discrepancy is an example of the method differences between the scopic forms of sugar testing.'],
    ['A urine dipstick shows a positive ketone test in a patient who fasted for 24 hours before a procedure.', 'The urinary ketone pad detects which ketone bodies, and which ketone is not detected by the standard nitroprusside reaction?', 'intermediate', 'A', 'Acetoacetate (and some acetone) but not beta-hydroxybutyrate', 'Beta-hydroxybutyrate mainly', 'The cholesterol molecule', 'The phosphate salt', 'The nitroprusside (Acetest-like) reaction detects acetoacetate and acetone but has little reaction with beta-hydroxybutyrate, so early ketoacidosis, which favors beta-hydroxybutyrate, may show a paradoxically weak result while the serum ketone increases. The discrepancy is a known limitation that urea or the newer quantitative methods resolve.'],
    ['A dipstick is positive for blood in a patient with a negative urine sediment; the specimen is from a female.', 'A urine that is dipstick positive for blood with no red cells on the sediment and a negative microscopy is most often explained by which cause?', 'intermediate', 'B', 'Bacterial nitrate', 'Hemoglobinuria or myoglobinuria (and an alkaline dilute urine lysing the red cells)', 'A reagent error', 'A high glucose', 'When the blood pad is positive but the sediment lacks red cells, the pigment is free hemoglobin (hemolysis or urinary tract bleeding that lysed) or myoglobin (rhabdomyolysis); an alkaline dilute urine can also lyse red cells in the tube. The pad detects the peroxidase of the heme group, so it reacts with both molecules rather than the intact cells.'],
    ['A urine with a pH of 5.5 and a friction-oriented sediment contains needle-like crystals recognized as uric acid; the physician relates them to a gout episode.', 'The most common crystal associated with uric acid stones precipitates in urine of which pH, and appears as what shape?', 'intermediate', 'C', 'An alkaline urine with envelope shapes', 'A neutral urine with square crystals', 'An acid urine with yellow or reddish-brown needle or diamond-shaped crystals (and rhombic plates)', 'An alkaline urine with coffin-lid crystals', 'Uric acid crystals are the naked coarse needles, rhomboids, or rosettes that form in acid urine (diet and gout), often with the color of the pigment urate; they precipitate below a urine pH of 5.5. The alkaline coffin-lid shapes are the triple phosphate (struvite) crystals, and the envelopes are calcium oxalate dihydrate.'],
    ['A urine sediment review finds abundant envelope-shaped (octahedral) crystals in a patient recovering from ethylene glycol intoxication.', 'Calcium oxalate dihydrate crystals appear as which shape and are seen in which clinical context?', 'easy', 'B', 'Amorphous clumps', 'Envelope (pyramidal) shapes, classic in ethylene glycol poisoning and hyperoxaluria', 'Sheaves of needle with initials', 'Sarcina bundles', 'Calcium oxalate dihydrate forms the envelope crystals whose presence with an anion gap acidosis and a high osmolal gap strongly points to ethylene glycol ingestion; the monohydrate form gives the dumbbell and the needle shapes. The other morphologies belong to tyrosine and urate crystals.'],
    ['A urine sample from a patient after a kidney stone episode shows coffin-lid (prism-shaped) crystals with a pH of 7.8.', 'The coffin-lid prism crystal formed in an alkaline urine is which compound, and with which clinical association?', 'intermediate', 'A', 'Triple phosphate (struvite) associated with urease-producing infections', 'Uric acid in gout', 'Cystine in metabolic disease', 'Cholesterol in nephrosis', 'Struvite (magnesium ammonium phosphate) crystals in a persistently alkaline urine suggest an infection with urea-splitting organisms (Proteus, Klebsiella) and stones (staghorn) formed by the alkalinization; the coffin-lid shape is the characteristic prism. Urate, cystine, and cholesterol crystals form in acid and specific settings rather than the alkaline infection.'],
    ['A child with a cystine stone has urine that has hexagonal crystals; the laboratory communicates with the pediatric nephrologist.', 'Hexagonal (benzene-ring) crystals in the urinary sediment are diagnostic of which condition?', 'intermediate', 'D', 'The hyperoxaluria state', 'The gout disorder', 'Calcium deposition', 'Cystinuria (and homozygous cystine stone disease)', 'Cystine crystals are the hexagonal plates with the typical refractive shape, seen in the hereditary cystinuria that recurs as stones, and the cyanide-nitroprusside test and the quantitative excretion confirm the disorder. The other crystals and findings have different identifiable shapes.'],
    ['A urine sediment in a nephrotic patient is examined and shows fat bodies with a Maltese cross pattern under polarization.', 'Fatty (oval) bodies that exhibit the Maltese cross under polarized light appear in urine from patients with which condition?', 'intermediate', 'B', 'Porphyria', 'Nephrotic syndrome (heavy proteinuria with lipiduria)', 'Hepatitis', 'Diabetes insipidus', 'The Maltese cross bright image under polarized light comes from the anisotropic cholesterol esters inside the tubular cells and the fatty casts, the signature of the lipiduria in nephrotic-range proteinuria; the finding supports the heavy glomerular protein leak. Porphyria and the parenchymal conditions do not produce the lipid bodies.'],
    ['A urine sediment contains a large number of red blood cell casts in a patient with hematuria and rising creatinine.', 'Red blood cell casts in the urine sediment are most characteristic of which renal process?', 'intermediate', 'C', 'The distal tubule of a benign process', 'Acute tubular necrosis', 'Glomerular bleeding (glomerulonephritis, vasculitis) with a normal finding early in the sediment', 'Bridge of infection', 'Red cell casts indicate that the blood originates in the glomerulus or the kidney, appearing in glomerulonephritis, IgA nephropathy, and vasculitis rather than the lower tract, where the casts lyse as they travel; the dysmorphic red cells and the proteinuria accompany the casts. Lower-tract bleeding cannot form casts because the cast needs the concentrated tubular environment.'],
    ['A patient with acute pyelonephritis has a urine sediment with white blood cell casts and a positive nitrite.', 'White blood cell casts in the urine are most significantly indicative of which condition?', 'intermediate', 'A', 'Renal (upper urinary tract) inflammation such as pyelonephritis and interstitial nephritis', 'A lower urinary tract infection', 'Normal pregnancy', 'A contamination artifact', 'White blood cell casts point to the renal parenchyma (pyelonephritis, interstitial nephritis, and acute graft rejection) because the cast matrix traps leukocytes in the tubule; a routine cystitis does not produce casts and instead shows white cells and bacteria without the cylinder. The distinction from a bladder infection rests on the casts.'],
    ['A technologist reviews a urine sediment and identifies broad, waxy casts in a patient with chronic renal failure.', 'Broad waxy casts in the sediment are associated with which pathology?', 'intermediate', 'B', 'An acute infection', 'A chronic, severe lesion of the tubules such as end-stage renal disease and acute tubular necrosis', 'A transient dehydration', 'A urine stone', 'Broad waxy casts form in dilated, nonfunctional tubules of advanced chronic kidney disease and acute tubular necrosis, and their broadness reflects the tubular dilatation with the waxy texture and few cells; they carry a poor prognostic association. The granular/coarse casts also appear in the nephritic picture, but the broad waxy form is terminal.'],
    ['A patient with minimal change disease has a urine protein of 8 g per day with a waxy and fatty sediment; the diagnostic workup is explained.', 'The peak protein pattern of a nephrotic syndrome and the finding of lipid and fatty casts support which glomerular disease pattern?', 'intermediate', 'D', 'IgA nephropathy', 'Glomerulonephritis of post-streptococcal', 'Thin basement membrane', 'Heavy proteinuria consistent with the nephrotic-range diseases (minimal change, membranous, focal segmental glomerulosclerosis)', 'A urinary protein above 3.5 g per day with edema and the lipiduria of fatty casts defines the nephrotic syndrome and its causes (minimal change, membranous, FSGS); the nephritic pattern instead shows the hematuria, red cell casts, and hypertension with a sub-nephrotic protein. The structural and laboratory distinction guides the biopsy.'],
    ['A urine specimen for protein is submitted with a request for a 24-hour collection; the total volume is noted to be 900 mL.', 'A 24-hour urine collection that is complete is validated by which additional laboratory approach?', 'intermediate', 'B', 'The pH', 'The total creatinine (and volume), because the 24-hour creatinine excretion is relatively constant and flags an undercollection', 'The glucose', 'The color', 'The 24-hour creatinine excretion (typically 15-25 mg/kg depending on sex and muscle mass) safeguards the completeness of the collection, because a low total creatinine means the aliquot is incomplete and the total protein result is underestimated; the volume and the collection length are checked against the local expected rate. The other measures do not validate the particular completeness.'],
    ['A urine albumin measurement distinguishes the categories of the albuminuria range in the kidney disease workup.', 'The dipstick and the urinary albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) are compared in which way when the clinical protein range is normal to mildly elevated?', 'intermediate', 'C', 'They are interchangeable in all ranges', 'The dipstick is more sensitive', 'The UACR is the more sensitive and quantitative method, so the microalbuminuria (30-300 mg/g) is tested by the albumin ratio and confirmed if needed', 'The dipstick always equals the UACR', 'The dye-based dipstick turns positive only at higher concentrations of albumin, whereas the albumin-to-creatinine ratio detects the low-range microalbuminuria and tracks the response to therapy; screening for the early diabetic nephropathy relies on the UACR and the timed value around 30-300 mg per gram. The dipstick represents an insensitive crude screen.'],
    ['A urine specific gravity of 1.010 is measured on a refractometer after a patient has been receiving IV fluids.', 'The urine specific gravity measured by a refractometer reflects the total solute concentration and correlates best with which measure?', 'easy', 'A', 'Osmolality (the number of particles), used as a surrogate for the hydration state', 'Albumin concentration only', 'The glucose only', 'The cell count', 'The specific gravity reflects the solute load of the particles, correlating closely with osmolality, and it falls with overhydration (near 1.001-1.003) and rises with concentration (1.030); the refractometer uses the refractive index of the dissolved solutes, while the dipstick estimates the ionic charge and may be affected more by nonionic substances. The osmolal relation is the principle underlying the interpretation.'],
    ['A urine dipstick pH is 8.5 in a patient without infection; the technologist reviews the specimen handling.', 'A urine specimen that sits at room temperature becomes progressively alkaline because of which process?', 'intermediate', 'B', 'Cell lysis only', 'Urea-splitting bacterial growth that converts urea to ammonia', 'Evaporation', 'Protein precipitation', 'Delay allows the urease-producing bacteria (and in some cases the endogenous urease) to hydrolyze urea into ammonia, driving the pH upward to 8 or more; the rise is the reason the fresh, refrigerated sample is needed for the crystal identification and the pH interpretation. Evaporation concentrates the specimen without alkalinizing it.'],
    ['A urine sediment reveals a large number of transitional epithelial cells in a specimen from a catheterized patient.', 'Transitional (urothelial) cells are a normal component of the urine sediment, but an increase suggests irritation to which structure?', 'intermediate', 'C', 'The glomerulus', 'The tubules', 'The bladder, ureter, or renal pelvis (the urothelial lining)', 'The prostate only', 'The urothelial cells lining the bladder, ureter, and renal pelvis shed into the urine normally; increased numbers follow catheterization, stones, and tumors of those sites, and the renal pelvis origin must not be confused with the epithelial casts that indicate the tubular injury. The finding localizes the process rather than the glomerulus.'],
    ['A urine sediment contains dysmorphic red cells in a patient with suspected nephritis; the microscopy is reviewed.', 'Dysmorphic red cells and acanthocytes in the urine sediment are a marker for which bleeding site?', 'intermediate', 'A', 'Glomerular bleeding', 'Lower urinary tract bleeding', 'Prostatic bleeding', 'Menstrual contamination', 'Dysmorphic red cells, including the acanthocyte forms with a membrane blebulae, appear when red cells pass the glomerular basement membrane and are distorted by the osmotic stress on the way; a high percentage supports glomerular disease (IgN, lupus nephritis, vasculitis). Uniform round cells in large numbers indicate a lower-tract source.'],
    ['A patient with an acute gout flare has a urine pH of 5.0; the nephrologist explains the stone risk.', 'The uric acid stone risk increases with a persistently acid urine because uric acid precipitates below which pH threshold?', 'easy', 'B', 'pH 8', 'pH 5.5 and below', 'pH 7.0', 'pH 7.5', 'Uric acid is largely insoluble below a urine pH of about 5.5, so the acid urine and the low urine volume allow the crystal and stone formation that the alkali therapy (citrate, 6.5-7.0) prevents; the pH and the hyperuricosuria are the joint risk factors. Alkalinization raises the solubility by converting uric acid to the soluble urate salt.'],
    ['A urine sample from a patient being treated for stones contains the peculiar envelope and the dumbbell shapes; the physician reviews the oxalate load.', 'Ammonium biurate (thorn-apple) crystals appear in which urine condition?', 'advanced', 'D', 'The acid urine of gout', 'The infectious alkaline urine', 'The nephrotic urine', 'The stale or decomposing urine and rarely in a chronically alkaline urine with liver disease (the thorn-apple spicules)', 'The thorn-apple crystal (ammonium biurate) often forms in urine that has stood too long and decomposed, and it can also appear in severe hepatic disease; the finding is more a product of the delay than a specific systemic disease in the routine setting. The other crystal shapes belong to urate, struvite, and oxalate.'],
    ['A physician orders a urine osmolality with the water deprivation test; the result is reviewed.', 'During the water deprivation test the urine osmolality distinguishes neurogenic from nephrogenic diabetes insipidus by which response?', 'intermediate', 'B', 'The total protein', 'The urine osmolality rises appropriately after the exogenous vasopressin in the central (neurogenic) form but not in the nephrogenic form', 'The glucose', 'The urobilinogen', 'After dehydration and the desmopressin dose, a central DI shows the urine osmolality rise to greater than 750 mOsm/kg because the kidney can respond to the added hormone, whereas the nephrogenic form fails to concentrate; the ability to concentrate the urine distinguishes the two sites, and the plasma vasopressin measurement adds to the diagnosis. The serum osmolality and the urine output support but do not separate the forms.'],
    ['A quantitative urine protein shows a total of 250 mg/g creatinine on an albumin-to-creatinine ratio in a diabetic patient.', 'The category of moderately increased albuminuria (microalbuminuria) by the KDIGO classification corresponds to which urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio?', 'intermediate', 'C', 'Less than 10 mg/g', 'Greater than 3000 mg/g', '30 to 300 mg/g', '0 mg/g', 'The moderately increased albuminuria (formerly microalbuminuria) is a urine ACR of 30-300 mg/g creatinine, while above 300 mg/g is the severely increased (macroalbuminuria) category and below 30 is normal-to-mild; the classification defines the stages of the diabetic kidney disease and the cardiovascular risk. Repeating with a first morning sample reduces the false positive.'],
    ['A urine specimen from a patient with strong-smelling urine is cultured and grows a urease-positive organism; the sediment is cloudy.', 'The clue to the infecting organism and the reason for the alkaline pH and the smell in a urinary infection is which metabolic activity?', 'intermediate', 'A', 'Urease splitting of urea into ammonia, which raises the pH and produces the characteristic odor (Proteus, Klebsiella, Morganella)', 'Lactose fermentation', 'Oxidase activity', 'Indole production', 'Urease producers split the urea into ammonia, making the urine persistently alkaline (with a pH often above 7.5) and predisposing to struvite stone formation, and the ammonia gives the strong odor; the organisms are the typical Proteus, Klebsiella, and Morganella. Lactose and indole features belong to other enterics, and the oxidase marks Pseudomonas and others.'],
    ['A poorly collected urine sample has a large amount of squamous epithelial cells and mixed flora; the culture is reported as mixed.', 'Squamous epithelial cells in the high-power field of a female urine specimen reflect which process, and what does the finding do to the culture?', 'easy', 'B', 'A real infection', 'Contamination from the urethra and the vulva, so the culture is likely contaminated with the mixed vaginal flora', 'A stone', 'A tumor', 'Numerous squamous epithelial cells indicate the contamination of the specimen by the surrounding skin and vaginal structures during the collection; the culture then grows a mixed flora or low-count organisms that do not represent a urinary pathogen, and a clean-catch midstream sample is advised. The finding warns that the culture result should not be overinterpreted.'],
    ['A urine sediment from a patient with a urinary tract infection shows clumps of bacteria with a positive nitrite; the technologist reports the findings.', 'The nitrite portion of the dipstick detects which organisms, and the principle relies on which reaction?', 'intermediate', 'D', 'Gram-positive cocci', 'Fungi only', 'The viral nucleic acid', 'Bacteria that reduce dietary nitrate to nitrite (the enteric gram-negative rods such as E. coli, Klebsiella, Proteus)', 'The nitrite test depends on the bacterial nitrate reductase that converts urinary nitrate to nitrite, so it is positive mainly with the gram-negative enterics (E. coli and relatives) and often negative with the gram-positive, Pseudomonas, and low-multiplication infections; the reagent produces a pink color by the diazo reaction. The specificity is high when the strip has nitrite present, but the sensitivity is limited.'],
    ['A urine dipstick shows a positive leukocyte esterase in a patient with a cloudy specimen but a negative nitrite.', 'The leukocyte esterase pad detects white blood cells in the urine through which mechanism?', 'easy', 'A', 'An esterase enzyme released by the granulocytes that releases a dye after cleavage of the reagent ester', 'A gram reaction', 'A light-scatter property', 'A specific antibody', 'The esterase pad detects the esterase secreted by the leukocytes in the specimen: the enzyme cleaves the indoxyl ester, and the released chromogen reacts with the diazonium to give a color graded with the number of cells. The absence of the intracellular esterase in the absence of white cells explains why the positive result with a negative nitrite points to a pyuria from any origin (including sterile inflammation).'],
    ['A patient with a suspected spontaneous stone is evaluated; the urine pH is consistently 7.8 with a recurrent infection.', 'A persistently alkaline urine with repeated cultures of urease producers and the demonstration of the vaccination-shaped struvite stones indicates which combination of management?', 'advanced', 'C', 'Only acidify the urine', 'Only a dietary change', 'Culture-directed antibiotics plus the endoscopic removal of the infected stones and the acidification by acetohydroxamic acid when indicated', 'No treatment', 'The struvite (infection) stones develop in the alkaline urine of the urease-producing infection, so the management combines the antibiotic therapy of the organism, the surgical clearance of the stone load, and the urease inhibitor (acetohydroxamic acid) as an adjunct; simple acidification does not reverse the formed stones. The recurrence is the hallmark if the stone burden remains.'],
    ['A urine specimen for a pregnancy test on a female patient returns a negative result despite a documented pregnancy; the sample is very dilute.', 'A spuriously negative urine hCG result in a very dilute specimen is prevented by which practice?', 'intermediate', 'B', 'Testing the color', 'Confirming the adequacy of the urine (specific gravity and the creatinine) and repeating on a first morning or more concentrated sample', 'Testing only the beta', 'Adding a second test', 'A very dilute urine can contain too little hCG for the qualitative threshold, so the adequacy check (specific gravity and creatinine) and a first morning specimen reduce the false negative; quantitative serum testing confirms the equivocal results. The qualitative card has a fixed sensitivity, and the dilute sample falls below the cutoff.'],
    ['A technologist performs a microscopic examination of a urine sediment and identifies hyaline casts in an otherwise normal sample.', 'Hyaline casts are composed mainly of which protein and appear in which clinical situation?', 'easy', 'D', 'The fibrin polymer', 'Albumin and fibrin', 'The IgM molecule', 'Mucoprotein (Tamm-Horsfall protein), appearing normally after exercise, dehydration, and diuretic therapy', 'Hyaline casts are the cylindrical Tamm-Horsfall protein matrix that forms in the concentrated tubule and appears in normal urine and with stress, fever, exercise, and small proteinuria; they are faint and require the reduced light to be seen. Cellular and granular casts instead indicate a pathology such as the nephritis or the tissue injury.'],
    ['A urine sample from a dialysis patient shows many broad casts and a low protein; the diabetes and the hypertension history are reviewed.', 'The diffuse accumulation of the broad and waxy casts in the concentrated urine of an end-stage patient indicates which tubular change?', 'intermediate', 'A', 'Tubular dilation and the atrophy of chronic renal failure', 'An acute infection', 'A transient dehydration', 'A drug effect only', 'In end-stage renal disease the remaining tubules dilate and become nonfunctional, producing the broad waxy casts that fill the sediment; the finding accompanies the progressive drop in the GFR rather than an infection or the reversible dehydration. The broadness marks the tubular diameter rather than the absolute quantity of the urinary protein.'],
    ['A patient with acute tubular necrosis after a hypotensive episode has a urine with a low specific gravity and many muddy brown casts.', 'The muddy brown granular cast picture in the early diuretic phase of acute tubular necrosis indicates what stage of the injury?', 'intermediate', 'C', 'The recovery is normal', 'The injury has cleared', 'The ischemic injury that produces the tubular cells continuing to slough into the casts during the recovery', 'The injury is glomerular', 'The muddy brown cast is the classic sediment of acute tubular necrosis during the course of the injury, and their persistence with the low specific gravity and the evidence of the recovering sodium handling tracks the tubular recovery; the casts are the granular heme-laden debris from the damaged tubules. The glomerular and the interstitial diseases show different sediment patterns.'],
    ['A urine pH is 6.0 in a patient with a calcium oxalate stone; the metabolic workup is planned.', 'The urine evaluation for a calcium oxalate stone includes which determinations to separate the hypercalciuric causes?', 'intermediate', 'D', 'Only the pH', 'Only the color', 'Only the 24-hour volume', 'The 24-hour calcium, oxalate and citrate with the urine volume and the serum calcium and PTH to identify the hypercalciuria and the hypomagnesuria', 'The stone workup measures the 24-hour urine calcium, oxalate, citrate, uric acid, magnesium and volume, together with the serum calcium, PTH and vitamin D, to separate the absorptive, the resorptive and the renal hypercalciuria and to guide the preventive therapy; the oxalate and the citrate determine the crystallization risk. The individual parameters alone miss the combined risk.'],
    ['A fresh urine is noted to have a specific gravity of 1.030 in a patient with a restricted fluid intake and a previous normal result.', 'A urine specific gravity of 1.030 obtained with a refractometer in a concentrated specimen corresponds to an osmolality in what approximate range?', 'intermediate', 'B', 'Less than 300 mOsm/kg', 'Approximately 900 to 1100 mOsm/kg (maximally concentrated)', '500 mOsm/kg', '150 mOsm/kg', 'The refractometer reading of 1.030 corresponds to a urine osmolality near 900-1100 mOsm/kg, the upper range of the renal concentrating capacity after dehydration; the exact conversion uses the standard relation of the urine osmolality to the refractive index. The value confirms a normal concentrating ability rather than a dilute kidney.'],

];
